/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - A real signal has a conjugate-symmetric spectrum, X(−f) = X*(f): the negative-frequency half is the mirror image of the positive half and carries nothing new.
   - the integral runs over all τ and is taken as a Cauchy principal value, which steps symmetrically round the point τ = t; x̂(t) is in volts.
   - sgn(f) = 1 for f > 0, −1 for f < 0 and 0 at f = 0.
   - The analytic signal, also called the pre-envelope, is xa(t) = x(t) + j x̂(t).
   - The complex envelope removes the carrier: x̃(t) = xa(t) e−j2πfct.
   - Writing x̃(t) = xI(t) + j xQ(t) gives the canonical representation, x(t) = Re{x̃(t) ej2πfct} = xI(t) cos(2πfct) − xQ(t) sin(2πfct).
   - A receiver finds I and Q by downconversion: multiply x(t) by 2cos(2πfct) and by −2sin(2πfct), then low-pass filter each product.
   - A band-pass filter with real impulse response h(t) also has a complex envelope h̃(t), defined by h(t) = Re{h̃(t) ej2πfct}.
   - Some syllabi call the complex envelope the low-pass equivalent.
*/
