/* book exam traps: The Hilbert transform of cos is sin, not −sin. The −j factor delays the positive-frequency phasor by 90°, and cos(θ − 90°) = sin θ. | x̃(t) is not the analytic signal xa(t). The analytic signal still contains ej2πfct; the complex envelope has the carrier removed. | H(f) = −j sgn(f), not −j for every frequency. Negative frequencies get +90°, which keeps x̂(t) real. | The complex envelope depends on the reference fc you choose. The same tone has a constant x̃ about its own frequency and a rotating x̃ about any other. | In low-pass equivalents keep one convention for the ½. Dropping it, or applying it twice, changes the gain by 6 dB. */
