/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - An experiment has a sample space S, the set of all outcomes.
   - P(A|B) is the probability of A given that B occurred; it is defined only when P(B) > 0.
   - Rearranged, it gives the multiplication rule P(A ∩ B) = P(A|B)P(B).
   - If A1, …, An split S into disjoint pieces, the total probability of B is P(B) = Σ P(B|Ai)P(Ai), and Bayes' rule reverses the condition: P(Ai|B) = P(B|Ai)P(Ai) / P(B).
   - A random variable X maps each outcome to a real number.
   - The mean is μ = E[X] = ∫ x fX(x) dx (or Σ x pX(x)).
   - For the standard Gaussian (μ = 0, σ = 1), Φ(z) = P(Z ≤ z) and the tail is Q(z) = P(Z > z) = 1 − Φ(z).
   - Two variables have a joint PDF fXY(x, y).
   - For a function Y = g(X) that is monotonic and differentiable, fY(y) = fX(x)/|g′(x)| with x = g−1(y); if several x give the same y, add the term for each root.
   - The central limit theorem (CLT): the sum of n independent, identically distributed variables with finite variance tends to a Gaussian as n grows, with mean and variance equal to the sums of the means and variances.
*/
