/* book Worked example: Q: A Gaussian noise voltage V has mean 0 mV and standard deviation 2 mV. Find the probability that a sample exceeds 4 mV, and the probability that it lies between −2 mV and 2 mV.
   1. Standardize the threshold: z = (4 − 0)/2 = 2.0, a pure number (standard deviations).
   2. P(V > 4 mV) = Q(2) = 0.0228, about 2.3 %. Because V is continuous, P(V ≥ 4 mV) is the same number.
   3. For the band: z1 = (−2 − 0)/2 = −1 and z2 = (2 − 0)/2 = 1.
   4. P(−2 < V < 2) = Φ(1) − Φ(−1) = 1 − 2Q(1) = 1 − 2(0.1587) = 0.6827.
   5. Check: by symmetry 1 − 2(0.0228) = 0.9545 of samples lie within ±4 mV, the familiar two-sigma figure.
   Answer: P(V > 4 mV) = Q(2) = 0.0228; P(−2 mV < V < 2 mV) = 0.6827. */
