/* book Worked example 2: Q: A binary channel carries 1s with probability 0.6 and 0s with probability 0.4. A sent 1 is received as 0 with probability 0.05; a sent 0 is received as 1 with probability 0.1. Find the probability that a 1 is received, the probability that a 1 was sent given that a 1 is received, and the overall error probability.
   1. Givens: P(1) = 0.6, P(0) = 0.4, P(r=0|1) = 0.05, so P(r=1|1) = 0.95; P(r=1|0) = 0.1, so P(r=0|0) = 0.9.
   2. Total probability: P(r=1) = P(r=1|1)P(1) + P(r=1|0)P(0) = 0.95(0.6) + 0.1(0.4) = 0.57 + 0.04 = 0.61.
   3. Bayes: P(1|r=1) = 0.57/0.61 = 0.934.
   4. Likewise P(r=0) = 1 − 0.61 = 0.39 and P(0|r=0) = 0.9(0.4)/0.39 = 0.36/0.39 = 0.923.
   5. Error probability: Pe = P(r=0|1)P(1) + P(r=1|0)P(0) = 0.05(0.6) + 0.1(0.4) = 0.03 + 0.04 = 0.07.
   Answer: P(r=1) = 0.61; P(1 sent | 1 received) = 0.934; Pe = 0.07. */
