/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - A random process X(t) assigns a random variable to every time t.
   - The mean is μX(t) = E[X(t)], an average across the ensemble at one instant.
   - For two processes the cross-correlation is RXY(t1, t2) = E[X(t1)Y(t2)].
   - A process is strict-sense stationary when every joint distribution depends only on time differences, not on absolute time.
   - τ is the time gap in seconds; the same RX(τ) results whatever t is chosen.
   - A strict-sense stationary process is also WSS; the reverse holds only for Gaussian processes.
   - A stationary process lasts for ever with finite average power, so each sample function has infinite energy and no ordinary Fourier transform.
   - the integral runs over all τ; SX(f) is the two-sided PSD; the inverse is RX(τ) = ∫ SX(f) ej2πfτ df.
   - Setting τ = 0 in the inverse gives the power: P = RX(0) = ∫ SX(f) df over all f.
   - Pass a WSS process X(t) through an LTI filter H(f).
*/
