/* book Worked example 2: Q: X(t) = A cos(2πf0t + Θ) with A = 2 V, f0 = 1 kHz and Θ uniform on [0, 2π). Show that X is WSS, find RX(τ), its values at τ = 0, 0.25 ms and 0.5 ms, and SX(f).
   1. Mean: E[X(t)] = A ∫ cos(2πf0t + θ)(1/2π) dθ over one full cycle = 0, for every t.
   2. Product: X(t + τ)X(t) = (A²/2)[cos(2πf0τ) + cos(4πf0t + 2πf0τ + 2Θ)]. The second term averages to zero over Θ.
   3. So RX(τ) = (A²/2) cos(2πf0τ) = 2 cos(2π × 10³ τ) W, which depends only on τ: with the constant mean, X is WSS.
   4. RX(0) = 2 W, the power of a 2 V sinusoid (A²/2). At τ = 0.25 ms the angle is 90°, so R = 0. At τ = 0.5 ms it is 180°, so R = −2 W: samples half a period apart are exact opposites.
   5. Transform with the cosine pair: SX(f) = (A²/4)[δ(f − f0) + δ(f + f0)] = 1 W at ±1 kHz, two impulses whose areas add to 2 W.
   Answer: μX = 0 and RX(τ) = 2 cos(2π×10³τ) W, so X is WSS; R = 2 W, 0 and −2 W at τ = 0, 0.25 ms and 0.5 ms; SX(f) = 1 W·δ(f ∓ 1 kHz). */
